  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 325 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled conditions return today through \nSaturday with daily chances for showers and thunderstorms.\n\nLatest wx analysis shows an upper level trough over the Great Lakes \nregion with a ridge over western Atlantic, causing SW flow aloft and \na surface stationary boundary draped near the VA/NC state border. A \nfew showers and storms have popped up in the area this afternoon, \nbut overall PoPs remain low with only slight chc to chc PoPs for the \npiedmont and SE VA/NE NC portions. The latest CAMs and HREF \ncontinues to show isolated convection coverage with minimal heavy \nrain threat this afternoon and evening. The severe threat seems low \nfor today with only 20-25kt of 500mb flow and relatively weak mid- \nlevel lapse rates. However, a few isolated stronger tstms are \npossible with enough surface heating, mainly NW.\n\nThe boundary drops SE tonight and settles into interior SE VA and \nthe Carolina Coastal Plain by Friday, but remains rather diffuse. \nThere is a strengthening jet core over New England, but there \nremains some uncertainty as to how well the RRQ of this feature \ninteracts with the surface boundary. The 12z HREF continues to a \ndecent signal for locally heavy rain from showers/tstms with a \ncontour of a 30% chc of 3\"/3hr across eastern/SE VA and NE NC to the \nRichmond/Tri-City metro areas. WPC has expanded the Slight Risk of \nExcessive Rainfall slightly north, now covering the southern half of \nthe FA. Locally heavy rainfall is the main threat tomorrow, but a \nfew stronger storms are possible with damaging winds possible, with \n20-25 kt of SW flow aloft. SPC maintains a Marginal Risk of severe \nstorms for the entire area Friday. Showers/storms look to be \nscattered to widespread based on the latest guidance. PoPs are now \n50-70% for a large portion of the area after 2 PM into the evening. \nLocally heavy rainfall will be possible in any showers/tstms. Drier \nconditions arrive by Sunday with only low PoPs over interior SE \nVA/interior NE NC.\n\nSeasonally hot and humid today through Saturday with high \ntemperatures primarily in the mid to upper 80s, and nudging up to \nthe upper 80s/around 90F (mid 80s at the coast) Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot conditions prevail during the early to \nmiddle portion of next week.\n\nHot conditions return early-mid next week as a 1.5-2.0 st dev 500mb \nridge builds from the Central Plains to the Southeast. NBM high \ntemperatures Monday through Wednesday are primarily in the lower to \nmid 90s. However, 850mb temperatures are primarily 19-21C in the 12 \nz/27 EPS/GEFS, with similar values in the deterministic 00z ECMWF. \nThis along with 1000-850mb thickness schemes support highs in the \nmid to upper 90s. The 12z GFS continues to be even hotter, but \nremains an outlier. If the hotter temperatures are realized that \nwill mean deeper mixing, which should allow dewpoints to drop into \nthe upper 60s/around 70F during peak heating. Regardless, this would \nsupport heat indices into the lower 100s. A developing negative \nheight anomaly near 40N/50W may cause the eastern portion of the \nridge break down/retrograde later next week. However, Thursday \nlikely remains quite hot as well. Mainly dry with PoPs near to below \nclimo Monday through Wednesday. Chances of showers/tstms may not \nreturn until Thursday once NW mid-level flow increases as the \neastern flank of the ridge breaks down.",
